# How I Lost and Found My Scientific Creativity

![rw-book-cover](https://www.science.org/do/10.1126/science.zumbaak/abs/_20240315_wl.jpg)

## Metadata
- Author: [[science.org]]
- Full Title: How I Lost and Found My Scientific Creativity
- Category: #articles
- Summary:: The author reflects on the evolution of their scientific creativity from the joy of research as a Ph.D. student to the challenges faced as a professor. They describe how responsibilities and distractions hindered their creativity until a move to a new university reignited their passion for research. By slowing down, finding moments of inspiration in everyday life, and fostering a curious environment for themselves and their students, the author rediscovered their scientific creativity and now aims to guide the next generation of scientists towards nurturing their own creative thinking processes.
- URL: https://www.science.org/content/article/how-i-lost-and-found-my-scientific-creativity

## Highlights
- I was free as a bird. And that’s when my first real post-Ph.D. eureka moment struck—of all places, while I was at a gym walking on a treadmill, watching the rain fall onto a lawn outside. As I saw puddles form and coalesce, I was able to visualize similar processes that I had been trying to understand belowground for decades. It was a small mental lightning bolt in my tiny slice of the scientific world. But it was exhilarating because it was a type of thought I worried had permanently dried up. It was clear I needed to slow down and experience the rain more often! ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01hzbdkaa2g3abjftv66zrgzgd))
- And I know that the environments that spark creativity for one person might not work for someone else. But one key lesson I learned was that being hyperbusy in the first half of my faculty career was antithetical to creative thought. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01hzbdmbq0fk8aanm8g6ny8gnh))
- I now encourage my graduate students and postdocs to disconnect and take time out to think, to play, to have fun with their research. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01hzbdmjncgf74pzwwst19yx3d))
- And to make these things a permanent habit in their lives—even if measured in only small fragments of each day. I also tell them to pay attention to the conditions and places that foster their curiosity and ability to think deeply. Creativity is the lifeblood of our scientific work. By openly discussing it, I hope we can help the next generation of scientists avoid losing it along the way, as I did. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01hzbdmw6bfb7mj131xd19pvvq))


